n: 3.15.9
name: drop
kind: hub
is: What the study lets go: library one-shot calls for small digests (97 to 183 ns per 40 bytes); a cryptographic digest per minted node; 64-bit and 96-bit digests as identity; and choosing BLAKE3 for speed at node size, where one scalar compression costs 48 to 51 ns against 16 ns for direct SHA-256.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-hash-measurement

n: 3.15.9.1
name: no-library-call-for-small-digests
kind: finding
is: Do not route small digests through CommonCrypto's one-shot call or OpenSSL's EVP interface: they cost 97 to 183 ns per 40-byte digest against 16 ns for the same SHA-256 instructions called directly, and in a store of 16.8 million nodes a mint through CommonCrypto costs 212 ns against 44.8 ns.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.15.2.2 3.15.2.3 3.15.3.2
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-hash-measurement

n: 3.15.9.2
name: no-digest-per-minted-node
kind: finding
is: Do not give every minted node a cryptographic identity. At 10 million nodes per second direct SHA-256, the cheapest cryptographic digest measured, takes 45% of one core and 80 bytes of table per node beside the 40-byte record; 128 GiB then fills in 115 seconds, so nodes minted at that rate cannot all be kept in any case.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.15.4.1 3.15.4.2
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-hash-measurement

n: 3.15.9.3
name: no-64-or-96-bit-identity
kind: finding
is: Do not use a 64-bit or 96-bit digest as an identity. 64 bits gives a 0.39 chance of an accidental collision among 2^32 items and certainty at 2^40; 96 bits gives 1 in 131,072 at 2^40. A 64-bit value is fit only to pick a slot with a full comparison behind it. A 64-bit FNV-1a key, as the old runtime computes, falls under this rule.
status: inferred
conf: high
from: 3.15.4.4 3.15.5.1
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-hash-measurement

n: 3.15.9.4
name: blake3-not-for-node-size-speed
kind: finding
is: Do not choose BLAKE3 for speed at node size. One scalar BLAKE3 compression measured 48 to 51 ns for any input up to 64 bytes against 16 ns for direct SHA-256 at 16 and 40 bytes and 31.6 ns at 64 bytes, a ratio of 3.0 to 3.2 falling to 1.6, and SIMD across chunks cannot help a single compression. The first round's BLAKE3-class tree hash keeps a case only for large leaves hashed in parallel or verified in part.
status: inferred
conf: mid
from: 3.15.2.4 3.15.2.2 3.15.1.1
limit: No SIMD or official BLAKE3 was measured; the measured ratio of 2.4 at 4096 bytes is an upper bound.
at: 2026-11-24
by: r2/gap-hash-measurement
